<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis Drake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1540–<a href="/years/1596">1596</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sir Francis Drake</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis Drake (c. 1540–1596) was an English sea captain and privateer who raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s American empire. He completed the second circumnavigation of the world between 1577 and 1580, was knighted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, and was vice-admiral in the fleet that defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Spanish Armada</a> in 1588. His last expedition with John Hawkins failed to take San Juan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> in 1595, and he died of dysentery off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portobelo">Portobelo</a> in January 1596.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-drake.jpg" alt="Francis Drake" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcus Gheeraerts the Younger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gheeraerts_Francis_Drake_1591.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Pacific coast suffered raids by English and Dutch interlopers: <b>Francis Drake</b> seized ships at Callao in 1579, and a Dutch fleet under Jacques l'Hermite blockaded the port in 1624.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Francis Drake</b> left Plymouth with five ships on an expedition authorised by Queen Elizabeth I. Called a voyage of discovery but in effect a covert raiding voyage, it returned in September 1580 as the second circumnavigation of the world.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-13">13 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It repelled <b>Drake</b> in 1595, the Dutch in 1625 and the British in 1797, though the Earl of Cumberland held San Juan briefly in 1598.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-puerto-rico">Spanish Puerto Rico</a></li><li><blockquote>Babullah then claimed authority over 72 islands from Sulawesi to Papua, and received <b>Francis Drake</b> in 1579.</blockquote><a href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tavistock, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portobelo">Portobelo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/panama">Panama</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.55,-4.14&amp;year=1576" aria-label="Tavistock on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4455 408 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francis Drake"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4905 678h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4905 678h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.55,-4.14&amp;year=1576">What was here in 1576? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I must have the gentleman to haul with the mariner and the mariner with the gentleman. I would know him that would refuse to set his hand to a rope!&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to his crew at Port St Julian, Patagonia, 1578 &#183; original &#183; from the account of a member of the voyage, as quoted in William Wood, Elizabethan Sea-Dogs (1918) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12855/pg12855-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My Masters, I am a very bad orator, for my bringing up hath not been in learning…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to his crew at Port St Julian, Patagonia, 1578 &#183; original &#183; from the account of a member of the voyage, as quoted in William Wood, Elizabethan Sea-Dogs (1918) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12855/pg12855-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
